.TH SYNCMOUNT 1 "2026-11-07" "syncmount 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
syncmount \- end-to-end encrypted, eventually consistent FUSE filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B client
.I URL MOUNTPOINT
.RI [ client-options ]
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B server
.I URL
.RI [ server-options ]
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.I URL MOUNTPOINT
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.I URL
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B status
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ \-\-mount-id " ID" ]
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B debug-state
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ \-\-mount-id " ID" ]
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B debug-manifest-diff
.I MOUNTPOINT
.RI [ OTHER ]
.RI ( \-\-url " URL" " | " \-\-cluster " HEX" )
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B debug-seq-gaps
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ \-\-mount-id " ID" ]
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B debug-conntrace
.RI [ \-\-limit " N" ]
.RI [ \-\-mount-id " ID" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B syncmount
mounts one encrypted namespace as a local directory and keeps every client
converged through a stateless sequencer.
The client applies data writes locally, returns immediately, and sends
encrypted operations to the server in the background.
Namespace changes and
.B fcntl
lock changes wait for the sequencer's verdict before the call returns.
The server assigns a total order and broadcasts ciphertext; it holds no key,
writes no disk and neither sees nor verifies Ed25519 signatures.
All payloads are sealed with ChaCha8-Poly1305 under the cluster key; a build
may select ChaCha20-Poly1305.
Control frames are plaintext hints.
Conflicts are resolved deterministically by every client replaying the same
order.
.PP
A client that loses its transport unmounts immediately and exits with status 10.
There is no offline mode and no pending-operation replay: remounting reconciles
the local tree against the cluster, cluster wins, and local-only paths are
deleted.
.PP
The 16-byte cluster identity is derived from the normalised URL
.RI ( scheme://authority/prefix ).
Every client of one cluster must use the same URL spelling, because the URL is
part of the Argon2id salt.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B client \fIURL MOUNTPOINT\fR
Mount the namespace at
.I MOUNTPOINT
and run until the transport fails or a signal arrives.
.I MOUNTPOINT
must be an existing absolute directory.
The path of
.I URL
is the namespace prefix; it must match the server and every other client.
The scheme must be
.BR http .
An
.B https
URL is rejected at dial with
.BR TlsUnsupported ;
the client has no TLS stack.
.TP
.B server \fIURL\fR
Run the sequencer.
Only the path of
.I URL
becomes the namespace prefix; the host and port of
.I URL
are ignored.
The listen address is chosen by
.B \-\-listen
or
.BR \-\-port .
.TP
.B status
Print one line per running client from its status document.
.TP
.B debug-state
Print the full status document, which is the replay and lifecycle state a
running client publishes.
.TP
.B debug-manifest-diff \fIMOUNTPOINT\fR \fI[OTHER]\fR
Read the local manifest cache below
.I MOUNTPOINT
and print its entry count, sequence and epoch.
With
.IR OTHER ,
compare two caches and print the added, removed and changed paths.
The cluster must be named by
.B \-\-url
or
.BR \-\-cluster .
.TP
.B debug-seq-gaps
Scan the connection trace for missing sequence numbers and print the first gap,
the last gap and the total count.
.TP
.B debug-conntrace
Print the most recent frame events, oldest first.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Global
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " FILTER"
Set the tracing filter.
The value is a level name
.RB ( trace ", " debug ", " info ", " warn ", " error )
or a full EnvFilter directive.
Precedence: this option, then
.BR RUST_LOG ,
then
.BR SYNCMOUNT_LOG ,
then
.BR info .
An invalid directive is a startup error.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-format " FORMAT"
Render logs as
.B text
(default, alias
.BR pretty )
or
.BR json .
.TP
.B \-\-foreground
Accepted for unit files and container entrypoints.
Both modes already run in the foreground; the option is a no-op.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the software, protocol, frame, WCP, rsync and signature versions.
.SS Client
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-other
Allow every local user to access the mount.
Requires
.B user_allow_other
in
.IR /etc/fuse.conf ;
without it the client refuses to start.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-empty\-view
Accept a JOIN that offers no view above sequence zero.
Cluster-wins then deletes every local-only path; this flag records that
consent.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-file " PATH"
Read the master password from
.IR PATH .
The file must be a regular file owned by the current user with mode 0600.
One trailing newline is stripped.
.TP
.B \-\-key
Refused.
A password in argv is visible in
.B ps
and shell history.
Use
.B SYNCMOUNT_MASTER_PASSWORD
or
.B SYNCMOUNT_MASTER_PASSWORD_FILE
instead, or pass
.BR \-\-key\-file .
With neither variable set and no
.B \-\-key\-file
option, the client prompts on the terminal.
.SS Server
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " ADDR"
Bind
.IR ADDR
.RB ( host:port
or
.IR ip:port ).
Default:
.BR 127.0.0.1:8080 .
Port 0 asks the kernel for an ephemeral port; the chosen address is logged.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
Bind
.B 127.0.0.1:PORT.
Conflicts with
.BR \-\-listen .
.SS Status and debug
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit machine-readable JSON.
.TP
.BI \-\-mount\-id " ID"
Restrict output to one mount id.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Number of connection-trace entries to print.
Default: 100.
.TP
.BI \-\-url " URL"
Derive the cluster identity from this URL.
.TP
.BI \-\-cluster " HEX"
Use an explicit 16-byte cluster id, written as 32 hex characters.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_MASTER_PASSWORD
The master password.
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_MASTER_PASSWORD_FILE
A file containing the master password.
The file must be mode 0600 and owned by the current user.
Setting both password variables is an error.
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_RUN_DIR
Base directory for per-mount runtime state.
Default:
.BR /run/syncmount .
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_LISTEN
Listen address used when
.B \-\-listen
is absent.
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_LOG
Tracing filter used when
.B RUST_LOG
is unset.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Tracing filter used when
.B \-\-log\-level
is absent.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Base for the Ed25519 identity key.
Default:
.BR $HOME/.local/state .
.TP
.B FUSERMOUNT_PATH
Explicit path to
.BR fusermount3 .
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_REQUIRE_FUSE
Test-only: a FUSE test that cannot run is a failure instead of a skip.
.SH FILES
Paths below use the default base;
.B SYNCMOUNT_RUN_DIR
replaces
.BR /run/syncmount .
.TP
.B /run/syncmount/\fIMID\fR/lock
Single-instance lock, mode 0600.
.TP
.B /run/syncmount/\fIMID\fR/status.json
Atomically replaced status document, mode 0600.
.TP
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/syncmount/keys/\fICLUSTER\fR/ed25519.key
Per-client Ed25519 identity, mode 0600, bound to one cluster.
The base defaults to
.B ~/.local/state
when
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset.
.TP
.B /dev/fuse
The FUSE device; the client checks that it can open it before mounting.
.TP
.B /etc/fuse.conf
Must contain
.B user_allow_other
for
.BR \-\-allow\-other .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a clean unmount after a signal.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error or a second instance for the same mountpoint.
.TP
.B 2
Startup failure: bad URL, missing key, unusable mountpoint, bind failure,
missing prerequisites, or an unavailable status document.
.TP
.B 3
The audit log is corrupt and was retained for inspection.
.TP
.B 10
The transport failed or the client was kicked; the mount has been released.
A unit that restarts the mount must treat this status as final; see
.BR SYSTEMD .
.SH SYSTEMD
The client exits with status 10 whenever it loses the cluster.
A unit that restarts on failure would restart it into the same failure, so
list 10 in
.BR RestartPreventExitStatus :
.PP
.nf
[Unit]
Description=syncmount client

[Service]
Type=simple
User=user
RuntimeDirectory=syncmount-private
Environment=XDG_STATE_HOME=/home/user/.local/state/private
Environment=SYNCMOUNT_RUN_DIR=/run/syncmount-private
Environment=SYNCMOUNT_MASTER_PASSWORD_FILE=/etc/syncmount/master-password
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/syncmount client http://sync.example.com/sync /home/user/share
Restart=on-failure
RestartPreventExitStatus=10

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
.fi
.PP
.I User
selects the account that owns the mount and reads the password file.
Set
.I XDG_STATE_HOME
explicitly: a system service does not inherit the user's environment, and the
Ed25519 identity lives under
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/syncmount/keys/ .
.I SYNCMOUNT_RUN_DIR
must be writable by that user;
.B RuntimeDirectory
creates the directory and removes it when the unit stops.
The mountpoint must already exist and be accessible to the same user.
The password file must be owned by and readable by that user.
.PP
Statuses 1 and 2 are configuration errors and do not improve with a restart.
Status 3 needs an operator because the audit log was kept for inspection.
A clean unmount after a signal exits 0 and is not restarted by
.BR Restart=on\-failure .
The client does not reconnect on its own: after the network returns, start the
unit again.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start a sequencer and mount it on one host:
.PP
.nf
export SYNCMOUNT_MASTER_PASSWORD='correct horse battery staple'
syncmount server http://127.0.0.1:8080/sync --listen 127.0.0.1:8080 &
mkdir -p /mnt/share
syncmount client http://127.0.0.1:8080/sync /mnt/share
.fi
.PP
The same invocation with the implicit aliases:
.PP
.nf
syncmount http://127.0.0.1:8080/sync --listen 127.0.0.1:8080 &
syncmount http://127.0.0.1:8080/sync /mnt/share
.fi
.PP
Inspect a running client:
.PP
.nf
syncmount status
syncmount status --json
syncmount debug-conntrace --limit 20
syncmount debug-seq-gaps --json
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
The local data write is acknowledged before the cluster sees it.
The operation is queued in an in-memory outbox, not on durable storage; a
crash, disconnect or kick loses operations that were not broadcast.
.BR fsync (2)
and
.BR fdatasync (2)
flush the local disk and ask the uplink to flush its coalescer; they do not
wait for a cluster acknowledgement.
.PP
The outbox also has a replay-backlog window: it closes at 256 operations that
were sent but whose echo has not been applied, and reopens at 128.
While it is closed,
.BR write (2),
.BR truncate (2),
.BR setattr ,
.B xattr
and namespace calls return
.BR ENOSPC
before the disk or the sequencer is touched, and the uplink sends nothing new.
.B ENOSPC
here is backpressure, not data loss; retry after a pause or lower the write
rate.
Four-kilobyte random writes from one client sustain about 1,024 operations per
second on btrfs with zstd, and more on tmpfs; above that the write returns
.B ENOSPC
while the mount and the client process stay alive.
.PP
The window protects the producer only.
The server has a bounded per-session downlink (8 MiB or 512 frames) and kicks a
consumer that falls behind with
.BR QueueOverflow ,
including an idle client that cannot apply the broadcast stream fast enough.
The kicked client unmounts and exits with status 10; remounting recovers.
.PP
A transport failure, heartbeat timeout, kick or protocol violation unmounts
the client and exits with status 10.
There is no offline mode, no pending-operation replay and no durable outbox.
A remount is cluster-wins: local-only paths are deleted and differing content
and attributes are replaced.
Reads use the local tree and can serve stale data during the convergence
window.
Reads are allowed during
.BR JOINING ,
so a reader can see a mixed tree while cluster-wins is replacing entries;
writes return
.BR EROFS .
A local tree with no non-empty file and no usable baseline is the only case
that transfers every file
.RB ( empty-local );
all other joins pull the holder's manifest and use manifest and rsync
differences.
.PP
.nh
.BR mmap (2)
with
.BR MAP_SHARED ,
device nodes, Unix sockets, FIFOs and
.B O_APPEND
are best effort: they are accepted and do not fail because of the weak
cross-client semantics.
Shared mappings write back through the ordinary write path and participate in
last-writer-wins, but
.BR msync (2)
is local only, a remote invalidation can raise
.BR SIGBUS ,
and concurrent mappings on two clients are an uncontrolled race.
.B O_APPEND
is anchored at the local EOF and can interleave across clients.
A device node and a Unix socket are local kernel endpoints whose metadata may
be broadcast; the endpoint is not shared across hosts.
FIFO writes do not cross clients.
Only regular files, directories and symbolic links have real cross-client
semantics.
atime is not synchronized; ctime is assigned at apply time from the server
order and is consistent across clients; uid and gid are not mapped between
hosts; mtime is client-supplied.
.BR ioctl (2)
supports only a small
.B FS_IOC_GETFLAGS/SETFLAGS
subset mapped to setattr; other commands return
.BR ENOTTY .
.hy
.PP
.B flock
is local to one mount and never crosses clients.
.B fcntl
record locks are cross-client and strongly ordered: a cluster mount
negotiates POSIX locks, every
.BR getlk / setlk
reaches the deterministic byte-range table, every lock change is an ordered
operation, and the caller waits for its replay verdict.
A conflicting
.B F_SETLK
returns
.BR EAGAIN ;
.B F_GETLK
reads the table at the client's applied sequence, so it can be stale by the
convergence window.
The owner is the mount, not the process; the reported
.I l_pid
is 0, and blocking
.B F_SETLKW
returns
.BR EOPNOTSUPP .
A departed holder's locks are released by a signed
.B LEAVE
or by lease expiry, not by a server hint; a crashed holder's locks are not
reclaimed immediately.
.PP
Namespace operations are queued and the caller waits for the replay verdict;
a concurrent loser receives the losing errno and its local tree is not changed.
The wait costs one sequencer round trip and answers
.B ENOTCONN
on disconnect or after 10 seconds; the operation may still be applied later.
.PP
A joining client proves its key with
.BR JOIN_PROOF ,
sealed with the cluster key.
A member answers with
.BR JOIN_ACCEPT ,
which carries the keys it knows.
The receive path verifies every operation against that directory and fails
closed on an unknown key or a bad signature, so a wrong password is rejected
at JOIN when a member is online.
A cluster with no online member trusts its first client without verification,
so a wrong password is accepted there and creates an isolated cluster.
Key distribution forms a star around each verifier; a cluster of three or more
clients is not stable until every pair has exchanged keys.
A member that is online but silent for the two-second proof deadline also
fails the JOIN.
.PP
Above sequence zero a JOIN also needs a view. The server names an online holder
whose applied watermark equals
.IR current ;
the client pulls that holder's manifest, blockmap and the blocks its diff needs
over a relay session, then runs cluster-wins.
A solo client that has applied
.I current
or is one
.B LEAVE
behind may use its local manifest as a self view; the client refuses a self
view that does not describe the disk exactly.
By default, if no holder and no self view is available, the JOIN retries
after 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 seconds and exits 2 without deleting local files.
There is no snapshot transfer on this path.
.PP
The heartbeat starts only when the client reaches
.BR STEADY .
The server suppresses the stall threshold during JOIN, but its 15-second
heartbeat timeout still applies, so a JOIN longer than 15 seconds can be
kicked.
RTT is not reported: the control prefix reserves that field as zero, and the
stall threshold always uses the 300 ms fallback.
.PP
There is one sequencer and no leader election.
A server restart resets the sequence, draws a new epoch and forces every
client to resynchronise.
The server can rewrite
.BR seq ,
delay, drop, duplicate or reorder frames.
Clients detect their own gaps and epoch changes, but cannot prove a
cross-client reorder.
Server control frames
.RB ( KICK ,
.BR LEAVE ,
.BR WELCOME ,
.BR PONG )
are availability hints, not authority; the plaintext
.B LEAVE
is ignored, so it cannot release a lock.
.PP
There is no perfect forward secrecy, no key rotation and no revocation.
A leaked password decrypts past and future traffic and requires rebuilding the
cluster.
Every operation is signed with a random per-client Ed25519 key and verified by
every receiving client; the server neither sees nor verifies the signature.
A PSK holder cannot forge another member's operation without that member's
private key.
Key distribution is peer-mediated, so the guarantee covers only peers that
have exchanged keys (see the JOIN paragraph above).
The server sees frame headers, routing and control prefixes, sizes, timing,
connection pairs and client identifiers, not payloads or paths.
There is no built-in TLS and the client rejects an
.B https
URL with
.BR TlsUnsupported .
A conventional TLS-terminating reverse proxy cannot be dialed by the client;
if transport protection is required, use a local tunnel that accepts plain
HTTP on loopback.
.PP
Content chunks have two identifiers.
.I chunk_id
is
.B BLAKE3
of the plaintext chunk (at most 256 KiB) and is the logical identity used by
the manifest,
.BR DataMap ,
.B BLOCK_REQ
and the local chunk cache.
.I blob_id
is
.B BLAKE3
of the ciphertext and is a physical-integrity identifier.
Deduplication happens before encryption, by
.IR chunk_id .
Each 64 KiB envelope of a chunk is sealed with a fresh random nonce, so equal
plaintext produces different ciphertext.
This is plaintext addressing: no key and no nonce are derived from the content.
A cluster member who holds the index can tell whether two blocks are
byte-identical; the server sees neither identifier.
See
.I docs/known-limitations.md
section 4.3.
.PP
There is no independent fsck, broadcast log, multi-tenant routing or
peer-to-peer transfer.
Content verification is deferred; the watermark health check plus a remount
are the recovery path.
One process serves one cluster.
rsync uses 16 MiB segments; a segment boundary can lose one block match.
The
.I librsync_oxide
backend is a new, pinned dependency.
The design point is about 20 online clients, one million files and 4 GiB per
file; larger deployments are not promised.
The three-or-more-client key-distribution limit applies before this design
point.
.PP
The full semantic boundary, including the accepted limits and residual gaps,
is in
.I docs/known-limitations.md
in the source distribution; the adversary model is in
.IR docs/threat-model.md .
Paths never appear in logs or status documents in clear text; only an
eight-hex prefix of their BLAKE3 hash is published.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fusermount3 (1),
.BR mount.fuse (8),
.BR fuse (4),
.BR systemd.service (5)
